{"name":"Ramona","hex":"#8f9a88","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Ramona","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from Spanish.","A place in the United States:\nA census-designated place in San Diego County, California.\nA minor city in Marion County, Kansas.\nA town in Washington County, Oklahoma, named after the Ramona novel.\nA town in Lake County, South Dakota."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ramona"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ramona","blocks":[{"text":"Ramona is an 1884 American novel written by Helen Hunt Jackson. Set in Southern California after the Mexican–American War and annexation of the territory by the United States, Ramona explores the life of a mixed-race Scottish–Native American orphan girl. The story was inspired by the marriage of Hugo Reid and Victoria Reid."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Ramona_Helen_Hunt_Jackson_1884.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramona"},{"title":"Ramona (novel series)","blocks":[{"text":"The Ramona books are a series of eight children's novels by Beverly Cleary that center on Ramona Quimby, her family and friends. The first book, Beezus and Ramona, appeared in 1955. The final book, Ramona's World, was published in 1999. Two books in the series were named Newbery Honor books, Ramona and Her Father and Ramona Quimby, Age 8. Ramona and Her Mother received the National Book Award. Sometimes known as the Beezus and Ramona series, as of 2012, the books were being marketed by HarperCollins as \"The Complete Ramona Collection\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/94/Ramona_Quimby_Collection_box_set.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramona_(novel_series)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Helen_Hunt_Jackson_by_Charles_F._Conly.jpg/500px-Helen_Hunt_Jackson_by_Charles_F._Conly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Helen_Hunt_Jackson_by_Charles_F._Conly.jpg/1280px-Helen_Hunt_Jackson_by_Charles_F._Conly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Helen_Hunt_Jackson_by_Charles_F._Conly.jpg/500px-Helen_Hunt_Jackson_by_Charles_F._Conly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Helen Hunt Jackson around the time she wrote Ramona","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHelen_Hunt_Jackson_by_Charles_F._Conly.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Rancho_Camulos.jpg/500px-Rancho_Camulos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Rancho_Camulos.jpg/1280px-Rancho_Camulos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Rancho_Camulos.jpg/500px-Rancho_Camulos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rancho Camulos","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARancho_Camulos.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Wishing_well_-_Ramona_marriage_place_-_San_Diego_CA.jpg/330px-Wishing_well_-_Ramona_marriage_place_-_San_Diego_CA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Wishing_well_-_Ramona_marriage_place_-_San_Diego_CA.jpg/330px-Wishing_well_-_Ramona_marriage_place_-_San_Diego_CA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wishing well, Ramona's Marriage Place","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWishing_well_-_Ramona_marriage_place_-_San_Diego_CA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Ramona_Lubo%2C_a_Coahuilla_Indian_woman%2C_Cahuilla%2C_California%2C_April_5%2C_1899_%28CHS-4752%29.jpg/500px-Ramona_Lubo%2C_a_Coahuilla_Indian_woman%2C_Cahuilla%2C_California%2C_April_5%2C_1899_%28CHS-4752%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Ramona_Lubo%2C_a_Coahuilla_Indian_woman%2C_Cahuilla%2C_California%2C_April_5%2C_1899_%28CHS-4752%29.jpg/1280px-Ramona_Lubo%2C_a_Coahuilla_Indian_woman%2C_Cahuilla%2C_California%2C_April_5%2C_1899_%28CHS-4752%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Ramona_Lubo%2C_a_Coahuilla_Indian_woman%2C_Cahuilla%2C_California%2C_April_5%2C_1899_%28CHS-4752%29.jpg/500px-Ramona_Lubo%2C_a_Coahuilla_Indian_woman%2C_Cahuilla%2C_California%2C_April_5%2C_1899_%28CHS-4752%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ramona Lubo, after whom the novel was named, many claimed","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARamona_Lubo%2C_a_Coahuilla_Indian_woman%2C_Cahuilla%2C_California%2C_April_5%2C_1899_(CHS-4752).jpg"}],"sounds":{}}}